le by dividing by max(1, prediction_tokens) to avoid division by zero. But since the instruction says \"normalize gain by at least one prediction token\", that suggests if prediction_tokens is 0, we should treat as 1? Or maybe such case is invalid. But we can just use prediction_tokens as is, and if zero, raise ValueError? Better to follow the instruction: \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" So if prediction_tokens is less than 1, we can treat as 1. But data has positive integers. We'll implement as: gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call; normalized_gain = gain / max(prediction_tokens, 1). Then check parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nAlso, \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that case has \"threshold\" and \"calls\" and each call has required fields. Raise ValueError if missing.\n\nReturn result as dict.\n\nImplementation in Python function solve(case). Use standard library only.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON string with the implementation.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a string (escaped). No markdown code fences. The entire response must be a valid JSON object.\n\nLet's construct the Python code. We'll write a function solve(case) that performs the filtering.\n\nWe'll also include a main guard? Not needed.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    if \"threshold\" not in case or \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"missing threshold or calls\")\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be number\")\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be list\")\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"call must be dict\")\n        # required fields: call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable\n        for field in (\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"):\n            if field not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"missing field {field} in call\")\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        # compute gain\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # normalize by at least one token\n        norm_gain = gain / max(tokens, 1)\n        # decision\n        accepted = bool(parseable) and norm_gain >= threshold\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"accepted\": accepted,\n            \"normalized_gain\": round(norm_gain, 8)  # rounded\n        })\n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut note: The output schema requires \"normalized_gain\" as \"number\". Rounding is fine. Could also use float but rounding is safe.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We don't.\n\nNow, we need to escape this as a JSON string. We'll construct a dict with key \"implementation\" and value as the source string. Then serialize to JSON with json.dumps. But the response must be the JSON string directly. Since we are writing the response, we need to manually escape. Easier: I'll write the Python source and then manually escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. But to be safe, I'll write a small script in mind to produce the escaped string. But as a text response, I can just write the double-quoted JSON string with proper escaping. I'll do that.\n\nWe must ensure the implementation is a single st